@meta
id: 14-17
title: The match itself, and the last replacement
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-backref, sub-basic
par: 50
minutes: 6
vscode: regex replace with `$0`, then retype the whole replacement for the second run
open: incident.md

@brief
Incident reports are rendered onto the status page, and the renderer
turns anything in backticks into a link to the admin console. Every
order id and every batch id in this report needs backticks around it.
There are fourteen of them and they are all different, so the
replacement cannot be a literal.

The replacement side has a character that stands for "whatever the
pattern matched". Use it for the order ids. Then, for the batch ids,
find out what `~` means in a replacement and save yourself retyping.

Nothing else in the file is wrapped. The incident number in the title
is not an id the console knows about.

@keys
| `&` | in the replacement: the whole match |
| `~` | in the replacement: the previous replacement string |
| `\&` | a literal ampersand, when you want one |
| `\vord_\d+` | an order id, in very magic |

@hints
You cannot write the replacement as text because every id is
different. Write it as "backtick, the match, backtick".
--
`&` on the replacement side is the whole of what the pattern matched.
`:%s/\vord_\d+/`&`/g` wraps every order id. The batch ids want exactly
the same replacement.
--
`:%s/\vpyt_\d+/~/g` — `~` is the replacement you used last time,
backticks and `&` included, and `&` still means "this match".

@solution
    :%s/\vord_\d+/`&`/g<Enter>  :%s/\vpyt_\d+/~/g<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Forty-two keystrokes, fourteen ids, none of them typed.

`&` is the backreference to the whole match — group zero, if you like.
It is what makes "wrap every X in Y" a one-liner: quote all the
numbers, put brackets round every date, turn every bare URL into a
markdown link. Whenever the replacement is "the same thing, decorated",
reach for `&`. If you ever need a literal ampersand in a replacement,
escape it: `\&`.

`~` is stranger and less known. It is the previous replacement string,
re-expanded, so an `&` inside it still refers to the current match.
The habit it supports is the one in this lesson: get the replacement
right once on one kind of thing, then apply it to another pattern
without retyping it. The next lesson keeps the pattern and changes
nothing; this one keeps the replacement and changes the pattern. Vim
remembers both halves separately, and both are reusable.

@start incident.md
# INC-2041: refunds double-settled

## Summary

Between 09:12 and 09:40 the settlement worker ran twice against the
same batch. Orders ord_9101, ord_9104 and ord_9112 were refunded twice.
Batch pyt_402 was marked settled before the second run finished and
pyt_403 never started.

## Timeline

- 09:12 pyt_402 picked up by worker-1
- 09:14 worker-2 starts and picks up pyt_402 again
- 09:18 ord_9101 settled by worker-1
- 09:19 ord_9101 settled by worker-2
- 09:25 ord_9104 settled twice
- 09:33 ord_9112 settled twice
- 09:40 pyt_402 marked settled, pyt_403 skipped

## Actions

- Reverse the duplicate settlements on ord_9101, ord_9104 and ord_9112
- Re-run pyt_403 by hand once the reversals are confirmed

